<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as KNAW, Royal Institute of Sciences, Literature and Fine Arts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> Academy of Arts and Sciences (KNAW) is the national learned society of the Netherlands, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. It was founded in 1808 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>, king of Holland, as the Royal Institute of Sciences, Literature and Fine Arts, and was reorganised as an academy in 1851. It advises the government on science and runs research institutes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The institution was founded during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a> as the Koninklijk Instituut van Wetenschappen, Letterkunde en Schoone Kunsten, the Royal Institute of Sciences, Literature and Fine Arts, by Lodewijk Napoleon on 4 May 1808. After the occupation had ended it was renamed in 1816 the Koninklijk-Nederlandsch Instituut van Wetenschappen, Letteren en Schoone Kunsten. In 1851 it was disbanded and re-established as the Koninklijke Akademie van Wetenschappen, and in 1938 it received its present name. Since 1812 it has been housed in the Trippenhuis, a 17th-century building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. It was awarded the Gouden Ganzenveer in 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Members are appointed for life by co-optation, and nominations from persons or organisations outside the academy are accepted; the criterion is scientific achievement. Besides regular members there are foreign and corresponding members, though no new corresponding members have been appointed since a new membership system was introduced in 2011. The academy has two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> with around 500 members in all: Science, covering mathematics, physics, astronomy, life sciences and engineering sciences, and Humanities and Social Sciences, covering the humanities, law, behavioural sciences and social sciences. Each has its own board, and the highest body is the general meeting of members.</p><p>The academy advises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> government, parliament, ministries, universities, research institutes, funding agencies and international organisations on scientific matters, provides a forum for the scientific world and promotes international scientific cooperation. It is also an umbrella for research institutes, among them the Hubrecht Institute, the Meertens Instituut, the International Institute of Social History, the NIOD Institute for War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> and Genocide Studies, the Fryske Akademie and the Rathenau Instituut. It awards prizes including the Lorentz Medal in theoretical physics, the Dr Hendrik Muller Prize for Behavioural and Social Science and the Heineken Prizes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Young Academy, De Jonge Akademie, was founded in 2005 as part of the academy for researchers aged 25 to 45, with ten members elected each year for five years. It was modelled on the German Junge Akademie and in turn was a model for the Global Young Academy. In 2014 the academy also established the Society of Arts, Akademie van Kunsten, a society of artists from architecture, visual arts, dance, film, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>, literature, music and the performing arts, which began with 19 members and was meant as a place for debate about the value of art in society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis founded the <b>Royal Institute</b> and a national museum, introduced a civil code and took a sympathetic line toward Dutch interests.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
